[Event "Strategy: the pawn break, lesson"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "teaching"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Hc5NIk9A/BOe48Lw4"]
[FEN "r2q1rk1/pp2b2p/2n1bpp1/3p1p2/1PP5/P3PN2/3NBPPP/R2Q1RK1 w - - 1 14"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r2q1rk1/pp2b2p/2n1bpp1/3p1p2/1PP5/P3PN2/3NBPPP/R2Q1RK1_w_-_-_1_14"]

{ Welcome to pawn strategy. First plan: the pawn break — a pawn driven into his pawns so the lines rip open. It needs room to work, so take the room first. Push to c5. [%cal Yc4c5] }
14. c5 { Pawn to c5. Space first: his bishop on e7 is buried, since d8 and f8 are his own pieces and d6 now drops it to your pawn. The break is b5, and it is coming. [%csl Gc5][%cal Gb4b5] } (14. Qb3 { The queen leans on d5 as well, but she takes no room. He simply trades on c4, and the tension is resolved on his terms, not yours. } 14... dxc4 15. Bxc4 Bxc4 16. Qxc4+ { Check, and an open position where you are better — but the space a pawn on c5 locks in for good has gone. [%cal Gc4c5] }) (14. Qa4 { The queen swings out to a4 and eyes his a pawn. He answers a5, and the queen-side pawns come off before you ever took the room. } 14... a5 15. b5 Ne5 { You are still better, and his knight settles on e5 while the clamp you were one move from never happened. [%cal Gc4c5] }) (14. Qc2 { A quiet queen move. It hands him the a5 square, which is exactly where your own pawns wanted to go. } 14... a5 15. b5 Ne5 { He took a5 first and his knight has e5. Better for you, yes — but a clamp was on offer and you let it go. [%cal Gc4c5] }) (14. Rc1 { The rook supports the pawn instead of pushing it. He answers dxc4, and the tension is gone at a moment of his choosing. } 14... dxc4 15. Qa4 a6 16. Bxc4 { The centre pawns come off and the board opens. Better for you, and without the space the push would have bought. [%cal Gc4c5] }) (14. cxd5 { Taking on d5 opens the long diagonal for his bishops. The point here was to keep the pawn and push it past. [%cal Rd5a8] } 14... Bxd5 15. Qa4 a6 { His bishop sits on d5 like a rock and the game drifts back toward level. [%csl Rd5][%cal Gc4c5] }) 14... a6 { He plays a6 to stop b5 for now. Bring the knight to d4, where it hits his knight on c6. [%cal Yf3d4] } 15. Nd4 { Knight to d4, hitting his knight on c6. If he trades, take back with the e pawn and it lands right beside c5. [%csl Gd4] } (15. Nb3 { Knight to b3 works too: from there it holds c5 and a5, the two squares this whole plan rests on. } 15... Bf7 16. Nfd4 Ne5 { Clearly better still: the knight reaches d4 anyway, one move later than it had to. [%cal Gf3d4] }) (15. Re1 { The rook develops. Nothing wrong with it — but the knight had a trip to d4 that improves your pawns as well. } 15... Qd7 16. Nd4 Nxd4 17. exd4 { The knight lands on d4 in the end, and you paid a move for the detour. [%cal Gf3d4] }) (15. Qc2 { The queen tucks in safely. Fine, and it leaves his knight on c6 holding his position together. } 15... Re8 16. Nd4 Nxd4 17. exd4 { The same knight reaches d4 either way, this time with your queen sitting on c2. [%cal Gf3d4] }) (15. Rb1 { The rook slides behind the b pawn, ready for the break. First things first: the trade on d4 is what gives your pawns their shape. } 15... Qd7 16. Re1 Rfe8 { Clearly better still, and the rook gained nothing for the plan — the knight still has the same trip to make. [%cal Gf3d4] }) 15... Nxd4 { He trades knights. Take back with the e pawn, so the new pawn on d4 props up c5. [%cal Ye3d4] } 16. exd4 { The pawn takes back and holds c5. Now for the break itself: a4 first, and then b5 runs straight into his pawn on a6. [%csl Gd4,Gc5][%cal Ga3a4,Gb4b5][%tail] } (16. Re1 { The rook comes to the middle — and his knight on d4 is attacking your bishop on e2. [%csl Re2] } 16... Nxe2+ 17. Rxe2 Bf7 { A whole bishop gone. Take the knight first: the e pawn recaptures and lands beside c5. }) (16. Nb3 { Offering a trade, while his knight on d4 is already attacking your bishop. [%csl Re2] } 16... Nxe2+ 17. Qxe2 Bf7 { The bishop drops for nothing. Recapturing on d4 was not optional. }) 16... Re8 17. a4 Bf7 18. b5 axb5 19. Bxb5 { There is the break. His a pawn is gone, and the pawn on b7 is the last one he owns on that wing — no pawn of his can ever defend it again. [%csl Rb7] } 1-0


[Event "Strategy: the pawn break, right away"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Hc5NIk9A/Bzn7Sw7H"]
[FEN "r4rk1/2R3bp/5ppn/p2pp3/4b3/1P2P3/PB1NBPPP/3R2K1 b - - 3 19"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r4rk1/2R3bp/5ppn/p2pp3/4b3/1P2P3/PB1NBPPP/3R2K1_b_-_-_3_19"]

{ You have been aiming at the pawn break e4 all game, and he keeps hitting your pieces to stall it. When do you finally push it through? }
19... Bf5 { His bishop lands back on f5, staring straight at your e4 square. Do you spend a move chasing it away, or push the break right now? } 20. e4 { Right away! Never mind the pawn — the hit on his bishop on f5 matters far more, and pushing now keeps the initiative on your side. [%csl Ge4][%cal Ge4f5] } (20. Bb5 { Bishop to b5 finds a nice square, but it stalls: the pawn break to e4 is still sitting there waiting to be played. } 20... Rfc8 21. Rdc1 Rxc7 22. Rxc7 { Better still — but the break never went in, and the bishop trip cost a move you did not have to spend. [%cal Ge3e4] }) (20. a4 { Pawn to a4, out on the wing, does nothing for the pawn break you actually came here to play. } 20... Rfd8 21. h3 Be6 { He tidies his position with the free move, and the break you came here to play is still on the shelf. [%cal Ge3e4] }) (20. Bf3 { Bishop to f3 attacks the pawn on d5, but he simply pushes past to e4, and his central pawns start rolling forward together. } 20... Be6 21. Nb1 e4 { His pawns roll forward together and e4 belongs to him now, not to you. [%csl Re4][%cal Ge3e4] }) (20. h3 { Pawn to h3 chases the bishop on f5 away first, but that gives him the time to push his own pawn to d4. } 20... Rfd8 21. g4 Be6 22. e4 d4 { He ends up with a protected passed pawn on d4 and the game is close to level — all for one move spent chasing a bishop. [%csl Rd4] }) 20... dxe4 { He recaptures with the d pawn. His king is one move from a check from your dark bishop — find it. } 21. Bc4+ { Check! The bishop swoops to c4 along the very diagonal his own capture just opened. [%cal Gc4g8] } (21. h3 { Pawn to h3 spends a move chasing the bishop, while the real business — the check on c4 — is still waiting to be played. } 21... Rfd8 22. Bc3 a4 { Clearly better still, though he gets the move to untangle while you were busy out on the rim. [%cal Ge2c4] }) (21. Nc4 { Knight to c4 heads straight for the corner, hunting the rook on a8. } 21... Rf7 22. Nb6 Rxc7 23. Nxa8 { An even trade of rooks rather than a win of material — the knight comes home through b6, and his pawns on a5 and e4 stay weak. [%csl Ra8] }) (21. Nxe4 { Knight takes back the pawn on e4, but he simply recaptures with the bishop, and now the bishop pair is his instead of yours. } 21... Bxe4 22. Rdd7 Nf5 { Level now: the bishop pair you handed over was worth as much as the pawn. [%csl Re4] }) (21. Bc3 { The bishop leans on the pawn on a5. Useful — but it is his king that is caught in the open, and only the check keeps it there. } 21... a4 22. bxa4 Rfc8 { The a pawn falls, and his rook reaches c8 in time to hold everything together. [%cal Ge2c4] }) 21... Kh8 { He tucks his king into the corner on h8, safe from the check. Now bring the knight into the attack. } 22. Nf1 { Knight to f1, heading for e3: from there it attacks the pawn on e4 and eyes f5 as well. [%cal Gf1e3,Ge3f5][%csl Re4] } (22. h3 { Pawn to h3 is slow: it does nothing for the knight's trip to e3. } 22... Rfd8 23. Bc3 Bf8 { A useful little move, and he spends the free tempo tidying up while your knight waits at home. [%cal Gd2f1] }) (22. Bc3 { Bishop to c3 attacks the pawn on a5 too, but he simply trades a pair of rooks and eases the pressure. } 22... Rfd8 23. Re1 Rd7 { The bishop hits a5 either way, and the rook trade lets him breathe. [%cal Gd2f1] }) (22. Re1 { Rook to e1 also attacks the pawn on e4, but he simply trades off a pair of rooks and simplifies toward the position he wants. } 22... Rfc8 23. Rxc8+ Rxc8 { Trades suit him: with fewer pieces on the board your pressure drains away. [%cal Gd2f1] }) (22. a4 { Pawn to a4 is a quiet move out on the wing, far from where the real fight over e4 is happening. } 22... Rfd8 23. Bc3 Ng4 { Harmless enough, and his knight jumps into g4 while your attention was out on the wing. [%cal Gd2f1] }) 1-0


[Event "Strategy: the pawn break, open the file"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Hc5NIk9A/BSRC5LSZ"]
[FEN "r6r/4kp1p/p2b1p2/2pp4/4p3/1P2P2P/P2PNPP1/2R2RK1 w - - 2 17"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r6r/4kp1p/p2b1p2/2pp4/4p3/1P2P2P/P2PNPP1/2R2RK1_w_-_-_2_17"]

{ His doubled pawns on the f column are a weakness in name only, until a pawn break opens a column onto them. Which pawn moves? [%csl Rf6,Rf7] }
17. f3 { Pawn to f3 attacks his pawn on e4 head on, and once the trade happens the f column opens straight onto his doubled pawns. [%cal Gf3e4,Gf1f7][%csl Rf7][%tail] } (17. f4 { Pawn to f4 closes the position instead of opening it, and his doubled pawns sit safe behind the wall for good. } 17... f5 18. Rc3 Rhc8 { Better for you, and his doubled pawns sit safe behind that wall for the rest of the game. [%csl Rf4][%cal Gf2f3] }) (17. Rfd1 { Rook to d1 develops, but the whole point here was opening the f column onto his weak pawns — this does nothing for that. } 17... Rac8 18. d3 f5 { Clearly better still — but the column never opened, so his weak pawns were never actually attacked. [%cal Gf2f3] }) (17. Rc3 { Rook to c3 lifts toward the kingside, but he simply trades a pair of pawns and eases his defence. } 17... a5 18. Rfc1 Rhb8 { Clearly better, though letting him trade first makes those doubled pawns far easier to defend. [%cal Gf2f3] }) (17. g3 { Pawn to g3 gives your king some air, but it spends a move while his doubled pawns sit completely untouched. } 17... f5 18. Rfd1 Rac8 { A little air for your king, and a free move for him to start sorting those pawns out. [%cal Gf2f3] }) 17... f5 18. fxe4 18... fxe4 19. Rf5 { The rook lands on f5, deep in his camp: the pawns on f7 and d5 are both in range, one down the column and one along the row. [%csl Rf7,Rd5] } 1-0


[Event "Strategy: the pawn break, do not prepare it"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Hc5NIk9A/wriejx5M"]
[FEN "r2q1rk1/pb1nbppp/1p1ppn2/2p5/2PP4/1PN1PN2/PB2BPPP/R2Q1RK1 w - - 3 10"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r2q1rk1/pb1nbppp/1p1ppn2/2p5/2PP4/1PN1PN2/PB2BPPP/R2Q1RK1_w_-_-_3_10"]

{ You have a central pawn break sitting on d5, ready to play. Do you prepare it first, or push it right now? }
10. d5 { Play the pawn break right now! Pushing to d5 cramps his whole position, and e4 comes next — wait to prepare it, and he gets time to stop it. [%csl Gd5][%cal Ge3e4][%tail] } (10. Qc2 { Queen to c2 prepares the break you could simply play right now. } 10... cxd4 11. Nxd4 Rc8 { He trades on d4 first and the tension is gone. Level now — the break was worth far more than another move of preparation. [%cal Gd4d5] }) (10. Rc1 { Rook to c1 develops soundly, but he simply trades on d4 first, taking some of the sting out of the break. } 10... a6 11. Bd3 cxd4 { A sound developing move, and he trades on d4 before you ever push. The break has lost its bite. [%cal Gd4d5] }) (10. Re1 { Rook to e1 is slow: the break is ready to play right now, and this move does not play it. } 10... a6 11. Qc2 cxd4 { The break arrives a move after it was ready, and by then he has taken the sting out of it. [%cal Gd4d5] }) (10. dxc5 { Taking on c5 releases the central tension for nothing: his knight simply recaptures onto a strong post. } 10... Nxc5 11. Qc2 Rc8 { His knight lands on c5 and the game is level. Releasing the tension yourself gave away everything you had. [%csl Gc5][%cal Gd4d5] }) (10. h3 { Pawn to h3 shuffles on the rim, far from where this game will actually be decided. } 10... a6 11. Rc1 Re8 { Harmless in itself, and it hands him exactly the tempo he needs to meet the break. [%cal Gd4d5] }) 10... h6 11. e4 { And there it is: pawn to e4. Three pawns stand abreast on c4, d5 and e4, and every black piece is boxed in behind that wall. [%csl Gc4,Gd5,Ge4] } 1-0


[Event "Strategy: the isolani, lesson"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "teaching"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Hc5NIk9A/Ofcz2Nc4"]
[FEN "r1bq1rk1/4bppp/p1n1pn2/1p6/3P4/P1N2N2/BP3PPP/R1BQR1K1 w - - 0 13"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bq1rk1/4bppp/p1n1pn2/1p6/3P4/P1N2N2/BP3PPP/R1BQR1K1_w_-_-_0_13"]

{ New plan: the isolani, an isolated pawn with no friend on either neighbouring column. Weak in the endgame, strong right now. His knight left d5. Push! [%cal Yd4d5] }
13. d5 { The break! With his knight gone from d5, the pawn advances there attacking the knight on c6 and the pawn on e6, and every black piece is still stuck at home to meet it. [%csl Gd5] } (13. Qd3 { Queen to d3 prepares the same break, but he gets there first with his own break, b4. } 13... Bb7 14. Bg5 b4 { His own break lands first, and the push you were saving is gone. You keep a small edge and nothing more. [%cal Gd4d5] }) (13. Bg5 { Bishop to g5 develops naturally, but the moment for the d5 break passes while you do. } 13... h6 14. Bh4 b4 { The bishop is developed, and his break on b4 gets in regardless. A small edge instead of a big one. [%cal Gd4d5] }) (13. h3 { Pawn to h3 is slow: it gives him time to get set for the d5 break before you even play it. } 13... Bb7 14. d5 Nxd5 { He is ready for it now and recaptures cleanly. Push it the moment his knight leaves d5, not later. [%cal Gd4d5] }) (13. Ne5 { Knight to e5 jumps into the centre, but it leaves the pawn on d4 undefended, and he simply takes it. } 13... Nxd4 14. Be3 Nf5 { A pawn gone for very little: his knight shuffles back to f5 and your edge has shrunk to almost nothing. [%csl Rd4] }) 13... exd5 { He captures on d5 with the pawn. Take back with the knight, landing right in the heart of his position. [%cal Yc3d5] } 14. Nxd5 { The knight lands on d5, forking the bishop on e7 and the knight on f6 at once — a monster in the middle of his position. [%csl Gd5][%cal Gd5e7,Gd5f6] } (14. Bxd5 { Bishop takes back on d5 instead, but it invites a wave of trades that drains all the pressure away. } 14... Nxd5 15. Nxd5 Be6 { Once the trades finish the pressure is gone and the game is level. [%cal Gc3d5] }) (14. Qd3 { Queen to d3 is slow: it gives him a free move to organise his defence around the knight on d5. } 14... Be6 15. Ng5 Qd7 { Level now: he catches up in development while your queen looked for a square instead of pressing. [%cal Gc3d5] }) (14. Bg5 { Bishop to g5 pins the knight on f6, but he simply untangles and the pin comes to nothing. } 14... Be6 15. Rc1 Rc8 { Level: the pin never bites, and he untangles the knight at his leisure. [%cal Gc3d5] }) 14... Ra7 { He tucks his rook onto a7, ready to defend the bishop on e7 if the knight forks there. Now develop with a hit. [%cal Yc1e3] } 15. Be3 { Bishop to e3 joins in, aiming straight down the diagonal at the rook he just tucked onto a7. [%cal Ge3a7][%csl Gd5] } (15. Bf4 { Bishop to f4 also works: a different diagonal, but the same idea of piling every piece onto the attack. } 15... Rd7 16. Nxe7+ Nxe7 { A fine square for the bishop too, and the trade on e7 takes his one active piece off the board. }) (15. Nxe7+ { Knight takes the bishop on e7 with check, trading away the monster on d5 a move early. } 15... Rxe7 16. Bd2 Bg4 { You keep a small edge, but trading the monster knight away this early hands back most of what you built. }) (15. Qc2 { Queen to c2 develops, but he simply takes the knight on d5 and the central bind disappears. } 15... Nxd5 16. Qxc6 Bb7 { He takes your knight on d5, your queen takes his on c6, and the bind in the centre is gone. Level. }) (15. h3 { Pawn to h3 gives your king air, but it does nothing about the fork already coming on e7. } 15... Be6 16. Nxe7+ Rxe7 { A little air for your king. The fork on e7 still lands, and you collect less from it than you would have. [%cal Gc1e3] }) 1-0


[Event "Strategy: the isolani, keep the lid on"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Hc5NIk9A/Tn2qTkzD"]
[FEN "r1bq1rk1/pp2bppp/2n1p3/3n4/2BP4/2N2N2/PP3PPP/R1BQ1RK1 w - - 1 10"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bq1rk1/pp2bppp/2n1p3/3n4/2BP4/2N2N2/PP3PPP/R1BQ1RK1_w_-_-_1_10"]

{ His pawn on d4 is the isolani, isolated, and your knight on d5 blocks it cold. Prying it loose is his whole plan. Keep the lid on. }
10. Re1 { His rook lines up on the e column, adding pressure down the centre. Which of your pieces must never leave d5? [%csl Gd5] } 10... Bf6 { Bishop to f6 piles more pressure onto the pawn on d4, while the knight on d5 stays put — his pawn can never push forward now. [%cal Gf6d4][%csl Gd5][%tail] } (10... Nf6 { Knight to f6 abandons the blockade, and his break on d5 is back on the table. } 11. a3 b6 12. Ba2 Bb7 13. Bf4 { Slightly worse for you: with the knight gone from d5, his pawn is one push away from breaking through. [%cal Rd4d5] }) (10... a6 { Pawn to a6 is slow: it does nothing to keep your knight anchored on d5, and the blockade slips soon after. } 11. Bb3 Nf6 12. a3 b5 13. d5 { About level, and the lid is off: his pawn breaks through to d5 the moment the knight leaves. [%csl Rd5] }) (10... Bd6 { Bishop to d6 finds a nice diagonal, but it does nothing to the isolated pawn on d4 the way a bishop on f6 would. } 11. Bb3 h6 12. Bd2 Nce7 13. Ne5 { Slightly worse for you: White claims a strong knight outpost on e5, while your bishop on d6 never joined the fight against the pawn on d4. }) (10... Nxc3 { Knight takes on c3, trading away the blockader for damaged white pawns — but it also hands White the bishop pair and open lines toward your king. } 11. bxc3 b6 12. h4 Bb7 13. Ng5 { Slightly worse for you: his pieces come storming at your king, and the doubled pawns you won turn out to matter less than the safety you gave up. }) (10... h6 { Pawn to h6 gives your king a little air, but it does nothing to reinforce the blockade on d5. } 11. Qe2 Bd6 12. Bd2 b6 { A little air for your king, and nothing at all for the blockade — he uses the free move to catch up. }) 11. h3 11... Nb6 { The knight shifts to b6 and still covers d5 from there, while the knight on c6 and the bishop on f6 both hit the pawn on d4. It is going nowhere. [%csl Rd4][%cal Gb6d5] } 0-1


[Event "Strategy: the isolani, chase the bishop"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Hc5NIk9A/FMxJOcuY"]
[FEN "r2q1rk1/1p1bbppp/p1n1pn2/6B1/3P4/1BN2N2/PP1Q1PPP/R4RK1 w - - 5 12"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r2q1rk1/1p1bbppp/p1n1pn2/6B1/3P4/1BN2N2/PP1Q1PPP/R4RK1_w_-_-_5_12"]

{ He has the isolani on d4, and every piece is in place to push it through to d5. His bishop on b3 is what makes that push work — chase it away. [%csl Rb3][%cal Rd4d5] }
12. Rad1 { His rook joins the attack on d1, and the pawn break to d5 is in the air. Which of his pieces must you chase away first? [%cal Rd4d5] } 12... Na5 { Knight to a5 chases the bishop off the b3 to f7 diagonal, and without it guarding the push, the d5 break loses its bite. [%cal Ga5b3][%csl Rb3] } (12... Rc8 { Rook to c8 develops naturally, but it does nothing to stop the break, and d5 lands next move. } 13. d5 exd5 14. Nxd5 Be6 15. Nxe7+ { He is a little better: the centre opens before your pieces are ready, and the fork on e7 wins your bishop with check. [%csl Rd5] }) (12... Nb4 { Knight to b4 tries to go around the bishop the other way, but it heads nowhere useful. } 13. Ne5 Bc6 14. f4 { He is better now — his pieces roll forward while your knight sits offside on b4. [%cal Gc6a5] }) (12... h6 { Pawn to h6 kicks the bishop, but it simply takes the pawn back and rips your king side open. } 13. Bxh6 Nd5 14. Rfe1 { Much worse: a pawn is gone and your king side is torn open. [%csl Rh6] }) (12... Re8 { Rook to e8 is slow: it does nothing to stop the break, and playing h6 later walks into the very same trick. } 13. Rfe1 h6 14. Bxh6 { He keeps the edge, and the same sacrifice on h6 lands all over again. [%cal Gc6a5] }) (12... Qa5 { Queen to a5 comes out actively, but it does nothing to stop the coming break. } 13. Rfe1 Rad8 14. h3 { He is better: the queen trip spends exactly the tempo the bishop chase needed. [%cal Gc6a5] }) 13. Bc2 { The bishop retreats to c2, out of the knight's reach. Keep chasing: there is more to gain here. } 13... Nc4 { Knight to c4 forks the queen and the pawn on b2, so the queen must move and White loses a valuable tempo. [%csl Gc4][%cal Gc4d2] } (13... Rc8 { Rook to c8 also works, but the knight to c4 was already the stronger move sitting right there. } 14. Bd3 Nc4 15. Bxc4 Rxc4 { He keeps a small edge, and your knight reaches c4 a move later than it could have. [%cal Ga5c4] }) (13... Bc6 { Bishop to c6 develops, but it costs a tempo, and his knight jumps into the strong e5 outpost. } 14. Ne5 b5 15. Qd3 { Clearly worse now: his knight claims e5 while your bishop was busy relocating. [%csl Re5] }) (13... g6 { Pawn to g6 weakens the dark squares around your king for no real gain. } 14. Ne5 Bb5 15. Rfe1 { Worse for you: the weakening pawn move does not even stop the knight jumping into e5 anyway. }) (13... Bb5 { Bishop to b5 comes out actively, but it opens the d column for his rook. } 14. Rfe1 Nc4 15. Qf4 { Clearly worse: the d column belongs to his rook now, and your knight reaches c4 a move late anyway. [%cal Ga5c4] }) 0-1


[Event "Strategy: the isolani, take it on purpose"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Hc5NIk9A/KG53RfuA"]
[FEN "r2qkb1r/3npppp/p1p2n2/3p4/NpPP4/1P2PQ1P/P2B1PP1/R3KB1R b KQkq - 1 10"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r2qkb1r/3npppp/p1p2n2/3p4/NpPP4/1P2PQ1P/P2B1PP1/R3KB1R_b_KQkq_-_1_10"]

{ Your bishop is the good one here, and his knight sits far from d4. Is taking on the isolani — an isolated pawn — a price worth paying for that bishop's diagonal? [%csl Ra4] }
10... e5 { Yes! The pawn takes the isolani on purpose, and in return your dark bishop gets the whole road from b8 to h2. [%cal Ge5d4,Gb8h2][%csl Ra4] } (10... Ne4 { Knight to e4 jumps in, but he simply trades it off, and your position stays solid but passive. } 11. Bd3 Nxd2 12. Kxd2 e6 { Solid but passive, and you never get the diagonal that made taking the pawn worth it. [%cal Gf8d6] }) (10... Qb8 { Queen to b8 tucks out of the way, but it does nothing to contest the centre. } 11. g4 e5 12. g5 { His king-side pawns come storming forward while your queen sits in the corner doing nothing. [%cal Gf8d6] }) (10... e6 { Pawn to e6 is solid, and your dark bishop stays stuck behind its own pawns instead of taking the road to h2. [%cal Gb8h2] } 11. Bd3 Be7 12. e4 { He keeps the whole centre, and your bishop on e7 is boxed in behind your own pawns. [%csl Re7] }) (10... h5 { Pawn to h5 is slow, out on the rim, while the centre is where this game will actually be decided. } 11. cxd5 cxd5 12. Rc1 { A pawn on the rim while the centre decides the game, and he takes the centre for free. }) (10... dxc4 { Taking on c4 opens the position, but his bishop recaptures there with a hit on your queen side. } 11. Bxc4 a5 12. Rc1 { Much worse: the pawn on c6 hangs to his queen, and his pieces are already lined up to collect it. [%csl Rc6] }) 11. Qe2 { His queen steps back to e2, out of the way. Bring your bishop out to its new diagonal. } 11... Bd6 { Bishop to d6 takes the long road toward his king, and it minds your pawn on e5 into the bargain. [%cal Gd6h2] } (11... h5 { Pawn to h5 first also works: gaining space on the wing before developing the bishop. } 12. g3 Bd6 { The bishop still comes to d6 next move, just with the h pawn already advanced. }) (11... Be7 { Bishop to e7 chooses the wrong diagonal, and he simply closes the centre in response. } 12. g3 e4 { Your bishop watches from e7 instead of the diagonal you paid a pawn to open. [%cal Gf8d6] }) (11... Ne4 { Knight to e4 jumps in first, delaying the bishop's trip to d6 by a move. } 12. g3 Bd6 13. Bg2 { Fine, but slower: the bishop still reaches d6, just one move later than going there directly. }) (11... g6 { Pawn to g6 gives your king some air, but it weakens the square f6 for no real gain. } 12. g4 Qe7 { The hole on f6 is a target for the rest of the game, and the bishop still has to come to d6. [%cal Gf8d6] }) 12. g3 { He blocks the diagonal with g3. Now get your own king to safety. } 12... O-O { Castle! Your king reaches safety on g8, and now your rooks can swing to the open e column. [%csl Gg8] } (12... a5 { Pawn to a5 gains a little space on the wing before you castle. } 13. Rc1 O-O { Castle next move regardless: the pawn move cost nothing, since castling was always still waiting for you. }) (12... h5 { Pushing h5 goes for his king, but your own king is still stuck in the centre. } 13. Bg2 a5 { Chasing his king does little while your own is still standing in the centre. [%cal Ge8g8] }) (12... Ne4 { Knight to e4 jumps in, but he simply takes the pawn on e5, and the whole point of the isolani is gone. } 13. Bg2 a5 14. dxe5 { The isolani trades itself off, and the diagonal you paid a pawn for goes with it. [%cal Ge8g8] }) (12... Qe7 { Queen to e7 moves off the back row, but it lets him push c5 with real bite. } 13. c5 Bc7 14. Bxb4 { The pawn on b4 drops for nothing, and the bishop retreat never saved it. [%csl Rb4] }) 13. dxe5 { He captures on e5. Take back with the bishop, and it lands exactly where the isolani plan wanted it. } 13... Bxe5 { The bishop takes back and sits on e5, staring straight down the diagonal at b2 and h2. The isolani pawn was worth every bit of this. [%csl Ge5][%cal Ge5b2,Ge5h2] } (13... Nxe5 { Knight takes back on e5 instead, and the bishop stays home on f8 — the whole point of the isolani plan never arrives. } 14. cxd5 cxd5 15. Bg2 { Solid — and the bishop's diagonal was the whole reason for taking that pawn in the first place. [%cal Gd6e5] }) (13... dxc4 { Taking on c4 leaves your own bishop on d6 hanging to the pawn. } 14. exd6 { A whole bishop falls, for nothing in return. [%csl Rd6] }) (13... Ne4 { Knight to e4 jumps in, but it does nothing to save the bishop on d6, which simply falls. } 14. exd6 { The bishop on d6 falls for nothing, and the knight jump does nothing to win it back. [%csl Rd6] }) 0-1


[Event "Strategy: the isolani, the open column"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Hc5NIk9A/U3Gx6y2G"]
[FEN "2r3k1/1b3pp1/p4n1p/1p1pq3/2nN4/P2RPP2/1P3BPP/1Q3BK1 b - - 6 29"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/2r3k1/1b3pp1/p4n1p/1p1pq3/2nN4/P2RPP2/1P3BPP/1Q3BK1_b_-_-_6_29"]

{ His isolated pawn on d5 — the isolani — is a real weakness, and he has no dark-squared bishop left to defend it. Where do your rooks want to go? [%csl Rd5] }
29... Nd7 { He steps his knight back to d7. The c column is wide open — take it before he does. } 30. Rc3 { Rook to c3 claims the only open column, and your queen is coming to c1 right behind it. [%cal Gc3c8,Gb1c1] } (30. Qa1 { Queen to a1 tucks into the corner, but it does nothing to fight for the open column. } 30... Nd6 31. Bg3 Qf6 { Better still, and the open column is not yours yet: he can put a rook on it too. [%cal Gd3c3] }) (30. Bg3 { Bishop to g3 hits his queen, and she simply steps aside. } 30... Qg5 31. Bf2 Qg6 { Chasing the queen wins less than simply putting your rook on the open column. [%cal Gd3c3] }) (30. h4 { Pawn to h4 edges forward on the wing, doing nothing for the fight over the c column. } 30... Nf8 31. Qa1 Ne6 { A pawn on the rim, and the fight for the open column has not even started. [%cal Gd3c3] }) (30. h3 { Pawn to h3 is slow: it spends a move on luft instead of grabbing the open column. } 30... Nf8 31. Rc3 Nd2 { Safe enough, and he gets a free move to contest the column you wanted for yourself. [%cal Gd3c3] }) 30... Nf8 { He regroups his knight to f8. Now bring the queen up behind the rook. } 31. Qc1 { Queen to c1 completes the battery down the c column, and his knight on c4 is pinned in front of his own rook on c8. [%cal Gc1c8][%csl Rc4] } (31. Qc2 { Queen to c2 blocks your own rook's view down the column. } 31... Qe8 32. Qc1 Ne6 { The queen reroutes to c1 in the end, one move later than she needed to. [%cal Gb1c1] }) (31. Bg3 { Bishop to g3 hits his queen again, and she simply steps away. } 31... Qe8 32. Qc1 a5 { Chasing the queen costs him a tempo, and your battery on the column is still unfinished. [%cal Gb1c1] }) (31. Qf5 { Queen to f5 wanders off toward the king side instead of joining the column fight. } 31... Qe8 32. Qb1 Nd2 { An active square for her, and the line behind your rook stays empty. [%cal Gb1c1] }) (31. h3 { Pawn to h3 is slow, and his knight simply jumps into d2. } 31... Nd2 32. Qc2 Nxf1 { His knight lands on d2 anyway, and your battery is still a move from being built. [%cal Gb1c1] }) 31... Ne6 { His knight lands on e6, hitting yours. Now push the pawn that finishes the job. } 32. b3 { Pawn to b3 kicks the knight off c4 for good, and the c column belongs to you alone now. [%cal Gb3c4][%csl Gc3] } (32. Bg3 { Bishop to g3 hits the queen again, but he simply trades knights on d4 and gives some of the tempo back. } 32... Qf6 33. b3 Nxd4 { A tempo won, and the trade on d4 that follows hands most of it straight back. [%cal Gb2b3] }) (32. Bd3 { Bishop to d3 attacks the knight on c4, and he simply trades it off. } 32... Nxd4 33. exd4 Qe8 { A tempo won, and the knight trade steers toward the sort of position he is happy to defend. [%cal Gb2b3] }) (32. Ne2 { Knight to e2 retreats to safety, giving up the fight for the c column for a move. } 32... Rc6 33. h3 Qf6 { Safe, and it gives him the free move he needs to double his own pieces on the column. [%cal Gb2b3] }) (32. h4 { Pawn to h4 pushes on the wing, far from the c column where the real business is happening. } 32... Nxd4 33. exd4 Qe6 { The pawn push does not stop the trade on d4, and that trade suits him more than you. [%cal Gb2b3] }) 1-0


[Event "Strategy: the minority attack, lesson"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "teaching"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Hc5NIk9A/oAeT1bml"]
[FEN "3rrnk1/pp3ppp/1npqb3/3p4/1P1P4/2NBP2P/P1QN1PP1/1R3RK1 w - - 3 17"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/3rrnk1/pp3ppp/1npqb3/3p4/1P1P4/2NBP2P/P1QN1PP1/1R3RK1_w_-_-_3_17"]

{ New plan: the minority attack — pushing your two queenside pawns against his three to leave one of his pawns permanently weak. Push b5, and his c pawn is weak for good. First, stop his c5 break. [%cal Yc3e2] }
17. Ne2 { Knight to e2 guards the pawn on d4, so his own break with c5 no longer wins anything. Now your own break, b5, is free to come. [%cal Gb4b5][%csl Gd4] } (17. Rfd1 { Rook to d1 also works: the rook develops first, and the knight to e2 still follows right after. } 17... a6 18. Ne2 g6 { The same minority attack plan arrives, just one move later than playing the knight first. }) (17. Rfc1 { Rook to c1 eyes the c column early, but his knight jumps into c4 before you can stop it. } 17... Nc4 18. Bxc4 dxc4 { Still better, though you had to give the bishop up to shift that knight off c4. [%csl Rc4] }) (17. a4 { Pawn to a4 pushes ahead, but his knight still jumps into c4 first. } 17... Nc4 18. Nxc4 dxc4 { Still a little better: the knight trade costs a slice of your edge, and the plan itself survives. [%cal Gb4b5] }) (17. b5 { Pushing b5 right away is too soon: his own break with c5 is ready to answer it. } 17... c5 18. dxc5 Qxc5 { His break on c5 frees his position, and the pressure you were building never arrives. [%csl Gc5] }) 17... a6 { He plays a6 to stop b5 for now. Bring your own a pawn up instead: a4, then a5, and b5 follows. [%cal Ya2a4] } 18. a4 { Pawn to a4 loads the minority attack fully. b5 comes next, and however he recaptures, his c pawn ends up standing alone for good. [%cal Gb4b5][%tail] } (18. Rfe1 { Rook to e1 also works: the rook gets active first, and the minority attack still follows. } 18... g6 19. a4 Ra8 { The b5 break still comes right on schedule, just with your rook already active on e1. }) (18. a3 { Pawn to a3 only goes one step, which works but costs an extra tempo to finish the plan. } 18... Ra8 19. a4 Bd7 { You spend two moves getting the a pawn where a4 would have put it in one. }) (18. Rfd1 { Rook to d1 is fine too: the rook gets active, and a4 still follows right behind it. } 18... Ng6 19. a4 Ra8 { The very same minority attack arrives, just with the rook posted on d1 instead of staying home. }) (18. b5 { Pushing b5 without the a pawn's support loses the pawn outright. } 18... axb5 19. Nb3 Ra8 { Worse, exactly as advertised: the pawn on b5 is gone and the knight never wins it back. [%csl Rb5] }) 18... Rb8 19. b5 19... axb5 20. axb5 20... Rec8 21. bxc6 21... bxc6 22. Rfc1 { The rook joins in on c1, right behind the queen. Two pieces are already aimed at the lone pawn on c6, with no black pawn left anywhere near to defend it. [%csl Rc6] } 1-0


[Event "Strategy: the minority attack, or the centre"]
