[FEN "r4bk1/rbpn1p2/1p2qnpp/1P1p4/3P4/2NBP2P/1BQN1PP1/1R2R1K1 w - - 8 21"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r4bk1/rbpn1p2/1p2qnpp/1P1p4/3P4/2NBP2P/1BQN1PP1/1R2R1K1_w_-_-_8_21"]

{ The minority attack has already done its work: his pawns sit on a7, b6 and c7. There is another break waiting in the centre. Which pawn plays it? [%csl Gd5] }
21. e4 { Pawn to e4! The centre blows wide open, and your bishop on d3 and rook on e1 both point straight at his king. [%csl Ge4][%cal Gd3h7] } (21. Rbc1 { Rook to c1 develops, but it leaves the centre closed and the e4 break still waiting. } 21... Bg7 22. Ne2 Ne8 { Still clearly better, and slow: the centre stays shut a move longer than it had to. [%cal Ge3e4] }) (21. f4 { Pushing f4 opens a different central lever, but it hands his knight the e4 square instead of you. } 21... Qe7 22. Nf3 Ne4 { Still clearly better, and the square e4 belongs to his knight instead of your pieces. [%csl Re4] }) (21. Rbd1 { Rook to d1 is slow: a developing move while e4 was ready to be played right now. } 21... Bg7 22. Qb3 Kh7 { Still clearly better: the position stays closed, and your bishop on b2 never wakes up. [%cal Ge3e4] }) (21. Nf3 { Knight to f3 develops, but his own knight simply jumps into e4 first. } 21... Ne4 22. Bxe4 dxe4 { Better still, though e4 is his square now rather than yours. [%csl Re4] }) 21... Nh5 { His knight jumps to the rim on h5, a poor square. Take the pawn on d5 now. } 22. Nxd5 { Knight takes on d5! His bishop must recapture, and then your pawn retakes, landing a monster on d5. [%cal Gb7d5,Ge4d5] } (22. Be2 { Bishop to e2 also works: it eyes the knight on h5 first, before taking on d5. } 22... Ng7 23. exd5 Qf6 { Winning still: his knight runs for safety and the centre opens exactly the same way. [%cal Gc3d5] }) (22. exd5 { Pawn takes on d5 directly: also good, the very same idea in a different move order. } 22... Qf6 23. Nf3 Nf4 { Clearly winning either way — the same monster pawn lands on d5, by the other move order. }) (22. Nf3 { Knight to f3 is slow: it lets him meet the eventual d5 push with his knight already posted on f4. } 22... Nf4 23. exd5 Qd6 { Clearly winning: the pawn still reaches d5, with his knight already settled on f4. [%csl Rf4] }) (22. e5 { Pushing e5 closes the centre again instead of taking on d5. } 22... Nf4 23. Nf1 c6 { Clearly better, and less than it could be: the centre shuts and his knight settles on f4. [%csl Rf4] }) 22... Bxd5 { He recaptures with the bishop. Now retake with the pawn. } 23. exd5 { A pawn sits on the monster square d5, his queen is hit, and the bishop on d3 stares straight at his king. [%csl Gd5][%cal Gd3h7] } (23. Rbd1 { The rook lines up on the d column — but his bishop on d5 is the piece that has to come off. } 23... Ba2 24. d5 Qe7 { His bishop slips away to a2, and your centre pawn has to run to d5 with nothing behind it. }) 1-0


[Event "Strategy: the minority attack, fix the weak pawn"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Hc5NIk9A/ASlEx2CT"]
[FEN "2r3k1/3q1pbp/1pn3p1/p2pPp2/3P1P1B/PQP2N2/6PP/5RK1 w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/2r3k1/3q1pbp/1pn3p1/p2pPp2/3P1P1B/PQP2N2/6PP/5RK1_w_-_-_0_1"]

{ Both sides carry a backward pawn from the minority attack you played earlier: yours on c3, his on b6. He wants to run his pawn to b5. Stop that, for ever. [%csl Rb6,Rc3] }
1. a4 { Pawn to a4 guards the b5 square for good, so his backward pawn on b6 can never advance again. [%csl Rb6,Gb5][%tail] } (1. Rb1 { Rook to b1 develops first, but he simply runs his pawn through to a4. } 1... a4 2. Qxb6 g5 { A messy fight follows, with his pawn sitting on a4 instead of frozen on b6. [%csl Ra4][%cal Ga2a4] }) (1. Qxb6 { Queen grabs the pawn on b6 right away, but it wakes his rook up onto the open b column. } 1... Rb8 2. Qa6 Bh6 { A clean pawn — and his pieces come alive on the open column to fight for it back. [%cal Ga2a4] }) (1. Re1 { Rook to e1 develops, but it lets him push his pawn through to b5. } 1... b5 2. e6 fxe6 { Level: he gets his own break in, and the backward pawn was never fixed at all. [%csl Gb5] }) (1. h3 { Pawn to h3 is slow, and it lets him push through to b5 all the same. } 1... b5 2. Qxb5 Nxe5 { Level: your queen takes a pawn on b5 and his knight takes one straight back. [%cal Ga2a4] }) 1... Nd8 2. Qxb6 { The queen simply takes the pawn on b6. A clean pawn up, and his whole queenside is falling apart. [%csl Gb6] } 1-0


[Event "Strategy: give a pawn, lesson"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "teaching"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Hc5NIk9A/NjC9EHjz"]
[FEN "rnbqk2r/ppppbp1p/6p1/3P4/8/4Q1P1/PP2PP1P/R1B1KBNR w KQkq - 1 9"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/rnbqk2r/ppppbp1p/6p1/3P4/8/4Q1P1/PP2PP1P/R1B1KBNR_w_KQkq_-_1_9"]

{ New plan: give a pawn, get the game — sometimes giving up a pawn buys you something worth more, like a wrecked pawn structure or a lead in development. One pawn push wrecks his for ever. [%cal Yd5d6] }
9. d6 { Pawn to d6! He is forced to take, and his pawns end up doubled on the d column for good — a pawn well spent. [%csl Gd6] } (9. Qh6 { Queen to h6 is also strong: it stops him castling king side. But it leaves his pawn structure untouched. } 9... d6 10. Nf3 Nd7 { Better still, and it settles into a normal game instead of the wreck the pawn push leaves behind. [%cal Gd5d6] }) (9. Nf3 { Knight to f3 also works: natural development, though it adds no extra pressure to the doubled pawns yet. } 9... O-O 10. Qh6 Bb4+ { Fine for you — and his pawns are never actually wrecked this way. [%cal Gd5d6] }) (9. Bd2 { Bishop to d2 develops, but it lets him castle into safety. } 9... O-O 10. Bc3 Bf6 { A natural developing move, and it hands him the move he needs to get his king safe. [%cal Gd5d6] }) (9. h4 { Pawn to h4 creeps forward on the rim, far from the real business in the centre. } 9... O-O 10. Nf3 Re8 { A pawn on the edge does nothing at all to stop him castling into safety. [%cal Gd5d6] }) (9. Qxe7+ { Taking the bishop on e7 walks the queen right into a recapture — his queen simply takes yours back, and you have given away a queen for a bishop. } 9... Qxe7 10. Nf3 Na6 { Lost: down a queen for a bishop, the pawn on d5 falling next barely matters. [%csl Rd5] }) 9... cxd6 { He is forced to take on d6. Now bring the knight around: to h3, then f4, then d5. [%cal Yg1h3,Gh3f4] } 10. Nh3 { Knight to h3, on its way to f4 and then d5. His pawns on d6 and d7 are doubled and weak for good. [%cal Gh3f4,Gf4d5][%csl Rd6][%tail] } (10. Bg2 { Bishop to g2 develops first. Fine, but the knight's road to f4 is still open whenever you want it. } 10... Nc6 11. Bd2 O-O { Better still: the bishop develops, and the knight's road to f4 is still open. [%cal Gg1h3] }) (10. Bd2 { Bishop to d2 is slow: development that does nothing to chase the doubled pawns. } 10... O-O 11. Bg2 Nc6 { Better still: he castles first, and your knight is still headed for f4 regardless. [%cal Gg1h3] }) (10. h4 { Pushing h4 storms the king side, but without the pieces behind it, it accomplishes little. } 10... Nc6 11. Nf3 d5 { The pawns advance with no pieces behind them, and he sorts the centre out at his leisure. [%cal Gg1h3] }) (10. Qd3 { Queen to d3 adds pressure to d6, but he simply develops and the pressure comes to nothing yet. } 10... Nc6 11. Bg2 O-O { A good square for the queen, and the pressure on d6 comes to nothing while he develops. [%cal Gg1h3] }) 10... O-O 11. Nf4 { Knight to f4: halfway home to d5, and his pawns on d6 and d7 are still doubled and going nowhere. [%csl Rd6,Rd7] } 1-0


[Event "Strategy: give a pawn, castle anyway"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Hc5NIk9A/9tZtwCFQ"]
[FEN "3qk2r/p2pb1pp/1p2p3/4PpB1/3Pb2P/5N2/PP2QPP1/2r2RK1 w k - 0 17"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/3qk2r/p2pb1pp/1p2p3/4PpB1/3Pb2P/5N2/PP2QPP1/2r2RK1_w_k_-_0_17"]

{ You are under real pressure: his rook wants c7 and his queen wants h5. Giving a pawn to castle is the price on the table — is it worth paying? }
17. Rxc1 { He recaptures on c1. Your queen cannot defend everything at once — what do you do now? [%csl Rc7] } 17... O-O { Castle! Yes, a pawn goes, but your king reaches safety and your pieces are finally free to work. [%csl Gg8] } (17... Bxf3 { Trading on f3 first removes a defender, but it hands his queen the active square f3 for free. } 18. Qxf3 Bxg5 19. hxg5 O-O { Playable, and the trade only gives his queen an active square on f3. [%cal Ge8g8] }) (17... Bc6 { Bishop to c6 guards the pawn, but your king stays stuck in the middle of the board. } 18. Bxe7 Kxe7 19. Qa6 { The pawn on a7 falls anyway, and your king is still stuck in the middle of the board. [%csl Re7] }) (17... h6 { Pawn to h6 kicks the bishop away, but your king still stays home in the centre. } 18. Bxe7 Kxe7 19. Nd2 { Keeping your king in the centre costs more than the bishop pair is worth. [%cal Ge8g8] }) (17... Qa8 { Queen to a8 tucks into the corner just to guard pawns — a passive square for her. } 18. Nd2 Bd5 19. Nc4 { Your queen ends up babysitting pawns, and your king never gets to safety at all. [%csl Ra8] }) 18. Bxe7 { He captures your bishop on e7. Recapture with the queen. } 18... Qxe7 { The queen recaptures. You gave up a pawn, but your king is safe and your other bishop is active on e4 — a fair price. [%csl Gg8][%tail] } (18... Bxf3 { Trading first, though that bishop was exactly what your pawn bought. } 19. Qxf3 Qxe7 20. Qb7 { His queen invades on b7, your pawns start dropping, and the bishop you paid for is off the board. }) 19. Rc7 19... h6 { You give your king a little air with h6. Nothing is hanging, and your bishop on e4 is worth every bit as much as his rook on the seventh row. [%csl Ge4] } 0-1


[Event "Strategy: give a pawn, keep what you have"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Hc5NIk9A/uI1zznqH"]
[FEN "r1bn2k1/1p3p2/p3pp1p/2p5/4P3/8/PPN1BPPP/4K2R w K - 0 17"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bn2k1/1p3p2/p3pp1p/2p5/4P3/8/PPN1BPPP/4K2R_w_K_-_0_17"]

{ You are a pawn up from the give a pawn plan a few moves back, and the temptation is to fix your doubled pawns right away. Do not — make something active of what you already have. }
17. O-O { He castles into safety. Your pawns are doubled and your own king already sits on g8 — what is the plan from here? } 17... Nc6 { Knight to c6 develops naturally, keeping the structure compact. The king walks up to join in next. [%csl Gc6][%cal Gg8f8,Gf8e7] } (17... Kf8 { King to f8 also works: the king starts walking first, and the knight follows right after. } 18. Rd1 Nc6 { The knight lands on c6 either way, just after the king starts walking instead of before. [%cal Gg8f8] }) (17... Bd7 { Bishop to d7 also works: it develops the bishop immediately instead of the knight. } 18. Rd1 Bc6 { Compact and safe, though the bishop ends up passive on c6 — the king walk is the real plan here. [%cal Gg8f8] }) (17... b5 { Pawn to b5 grabs space, but it lets his rook come into the open column. } 18. b3 Nc6 19. Rc1 { Still better, and his rook gets into the game. The extra pawn is worth more than the space. [%cal Gg8f8] }) (17... e5 { Pushing e5 fixes your doubled pawn, but it hands him the d5 square and a whole diagonal for his pieces. } 18. Ne3 Be6 19. Bc4 { His pieces come alive on the squares you just handed over, and the extra pawn is what mattered. [%csl Rd5] }) 18. Rd1 { His rook claims the open column. Now start walking your king up. } 18... Kf8 { The king steps up to f8. In an endgame the king is a fighting piece: it is heading for e7 and then d6. [%cal Gf8e7] } (18... Rb8 { Rook to b8 moves first, and the king's walk waits a move. } 19. Rd3 Kf8 { Still clearly better: the king simply starts its walk a move later than it could have. [%cal Gg8f8] }) (18... b5 { Pawn to b5 grabs more space, but it lets his rook land on d6. } 19. Rd6 Bb7 { Still better, and his rook landing on d6 costs you a slice of the edge. [%csl Rd6] }) (18... e5 { Pushing e5 gives up the d5 square all over again. } 19. Rd5 Be6 20. Rxc5 { A pawn goes back, and the extra pawn was the whole point of this ending. [%cal Gf8e7] }) (18... Kg7 { King to g7 heads the wrong way — in the endgame, your king belongs in the centre. } 19. f4 Kf8 { Still better, and now the king has to turn around and walk the other way. [%cal Gf8e7] }) 19. Kf1 { His own king comes out to meet yours. Keep walking toward the centre. } 19... Ke7 { The king comes to e7, heading for d6. In an endgame, an extra pawn is an extra pawn, ugly structure or not. [%csl Ge7][%tail] } (19... b6 { Pawn to b6 also works: it opens a diagonal for the bishop to reach b7. } 20. Rd3 Bb7 { Clearly better: the bishop gets a diagonal, and the king marches on just the same. [%cal Ge7d6] }) (19... b5 { Pawn to b5 is fine, though it leaves the pawn itself a little loose. } 20. Rd3 Ne5 { Clearly better: the knight finds e5, though the pawn on b5 is left a touch loose. [%cal Ge7d6] }) (19... Rb8 { Rook to b8 is slow: a rook move while the king still has not even started walking. } 20. Rd3 b5 { Still better even without the king's help — an extra pawn tells in the long run. [%cal Ge7d6] }) (19... Kg7 { King to g7 heads the wrong way again, away from the centre where it is needed. } 20. Rd3 b5 { Still better, and the king is on the wrong side of the board to help out. [%cal Ge7d6] }) 20. Rd3 20... Bd7 21. Rh3 21... Nd4 22. Nxd4 22... cxd4 { You recapture toward the centre, and the pawn on d4 is passed — no white pawn left on the board can ever stop it, and you are still a full pawn up besides. [%csl Gd4] } 0-1


[Event "Strategy: give a pawn, fix his pawns"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Hc5NIk9A/FrDFQwGc"]
[FEN "r1bq1r2/pp2p1bk/3p2p1/P2Pnp1p/4P2P/R1N1BP2/1P1QBP2/4K2R b K - 3 16"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bq1r2/pp2p1bk/3p2p1/P2Pnp1p/4P2P/R1N1BP2/1P1QBP2/4K2R_b_K_-_3_16"]

{ He wants to play f4 himself and free his cramped position. Give a pawn of your own to deny him that — one pawn move settles the whole question. }
16... f4 { Pawn to f4! His bishop takes it, but his pawns are now stuck on light squares for good, and the f4 square is never his to use. [%csl Rf3,Re4][%cal Gf8f7] } (16... Rf7 { Rook to f7 is a useful move, but it lets him play his own f4 break first. } 17. f4 Ng4 18. Bd4 { Level: his bishop settles on d4, and he gets the freeing break you meant to deny him. [%csl Gf4] }) (16... Bd7 { Bishop to d7 develops, but it also lets his f4 break come through. } 17. f4 Ng4 18. Bd4 { Level: his bishop takes d4, and the break you came here to stop arrives anyway. [%cal Gf5f4] }) (16... Nf7 { Knight to f7 retreats, but his own knight jumps into the strong b5 square. } 17. Nb5 fxe4 18. fxe4 { He is better: his knight lands on b5 while yours only went backwards. [%cal Gf5f4] }) (16... Bf6 { Bishop to f6 moves off its post, and he simply claims the diagonal it left behind. } 17. Bd4 f4 18. Nb5 { He is better: leaving that long road lets his bishop take d4, and his knight eyes a7 from b5. [%cal Gg7a1] }) (16... Nxf3+ { Knight takes the pawn on f3 with check, but the bishop simply recaptures, and the knight is lost for nothing. } 17. Bxf3 { A whole knight given away for a single pawn. [%csl Rf3] }) 17. Bxf4 { He recaptures on f4. Now the f column is yours to claim. } 17... Rf7 { Rook to f7: the f column belongs to you, and the bishop on f4 stands as a clear target straight down it. [%cal Gf7f4,Gd8f8] } (17... Bd7 { Bishop to d7 is also playable: the same exchange sacrifice on f4 still comes, just a move later. } 18. Nd1 Rxf4 19. Qxf4 Bh6 { Level: the exchange sacrifice on f4 still works, with your bishop already out on d7. }) (17... b6 { Pawn to b6 grabs space, but it gives him time to castle into safety. } 18. axb6 Qxb6 19. O-O { He castles to safety, and the pawn trade only opens the a column for his rook. [%cal Gf8f7] }) (17... Rf6 { Rook to f6 blocks its own bishop's diagonal, weakening the exchange sacrifice that follows. } 18. Nd1 Rxf4 19. Qxf4 Bh6 { He is better: from f6 the rook blocked its own bishop, so the same sacrifice achieves less. [%cal Gf8f7] }) (17... Rxf4 { An exchange sacrifice: your rook for his bishop, chasing activity and the bishop pair in return. } 18. Qxf4 Bh6 19. Qg3 Qf8 { He is better: the balance holds, and you are simply the exchange down for your trouble. [%cal Gf8f7] }) 0-1


[Event "Strategy: space, lesson"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "teaching"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Hc5NIk9A/LQLpaQfD"]
[FEN "r1b1r1k1/1pp1n1pp/p1pb1p2/8/4PP2/1NN5/PPP3PP/R1B2RK1 w - - 0 12"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1b1r1k1/1pp1n1pp/p1pb1p2/8/4PP2/1NN5/PPP3PP/R1B2RK1_w_-_-_0_12"]

{ New plan: space — a pawn push that cramps his pieces behind their own pawns, robbing them of squares to stand on. Trade his good bishop, then invade. Push the f pawn. [%cal Yf4f5][%csl Rc8] }
12. f5 { Pawn to f5! His bishop on c8 is buried with nowhere safe to go but d7, the square e6 is yours, and f4 is free for a bishop trade. [%csl Rc8,Ge6][%cal Gc1f4] } (12. a3 { Pawn to a3 is quiet, but it gives him time to get ready for the f5 push. } 12... b6 13. f5 Bb7 { Slightly worse for you: his bishop gets out to b7 first, before the space plan can shut it in. [%csl Gb7] }) (12. Kf2 { King to f2 tidies up first, but it does nothing for the space plan. } 12... b6 13. Re1 Bb7 { Slightly worse for you: without f5 to shut it in, his bishop gets out to b7 with time to spare. }) (12. Bd2 { Bishop to d2 develops, but it lets his a pawn run forward unchallenged. } 12... a5 13. Rae1 a4 { Slightly worse for you: his queenside pawns roll forward while the space plan waits. }) (12. h3 { Pawn to h3 is slow: it does nothing for your space plan while he gets on with his own. } 12... a5 13. a3 Bd7 { Slightly worse for you: his bishop reaches d7 in peace, since your h pawn move never challenged anything. }) 12... b6 { He wants his bishop out on b7. Trade his good bishop before it gets the chance. [%cal Yc1f4] } 13. Bf4 { Bishop to f4 challenges his dark-squared bishop, the better of his two, and offers the trade. [%cal Gf4d6] } (13. a3 { Pawn to a3 is slow, and it leaves him his whole bishop pair intact. } 13... Bb7 14. Bf4 Bxf4 { The trade on f4 still happens all the same, just a move later than playing it right away. }) (13. g4 { Pawn to g4 storms forward, but it skips the bishop trade the plan actually needs. } 13... Bb7 14. Bf4 Bxf4 { The bishop trade still comes right after, just following the pawn storm instead of leading it. }) (13. h3 { Pawn to h3 is slow: it stalls the bishop trade you already set up with f5, and gives him a free tempo. } 13... Bb7 14. Bf4 Bxf4 { The bishop trade still happens on f4, but the h3 move lets him develop his other bishop for free in the meantime. }) (13. a4 { Pawn to a4 gains a little space on the edge, but does nothing for the bishop trade. } 13... Bb7 14. Bf4 Bxf4 { The trade on f4 goes the same way regardless, and the pawn on a4 did nothing to help you get there. }) 13... Bxf4 { He captures on f4. Take back with the rook. [%cal Yf1f4] } 14. Rxf4 { The rook takes back on f4. His better bishop is off the board, and the one he has left is still buried on c8. [%csl Rc8] } (14. Rad1 { The rook takes the open column — but his bishop has just taken yours on f4, and it is still sitting there. [%csl Rf4] } 14... Be5 { The bishop steps to safety and you are a whole piece down. [%csl Re5] }) 14... c5 { He pushes his pawn to c5, taking the d4 square from you. Which of your pieces has an open road? [%csl Gd1] } 15. Rd1 { The rook takes the open column, and d7 is the square it is heading for. Cramp him first, then walk in. [%cal Gd1d7][%tail] } (15. Na4 { Knight to a4, where it eyes b6 and c5 — but a knight on the edge is a long way from the fight. } 15... Bb7 16. Rd1 Rad8 { His bishop reaches the long road to g2 and the rooks come off, while your knight sits out on a4 doing nothing. [%csl Ra4][%cal Rb7g2] }) (15. Nd2 { Knight to d2 heads for c4 by the longer road. Fine, but slower. } 15... Rb8 16. Nc4 Rd8 { Level, and the knight takes the long road — he gets a free move while it travels. [%cal Gd1d7] }) (15. Rf2 { Rook to f2 tidies up, but it leaves his queenside pawns completely untouched. } 15... Rb8 16. Nd2 Bb7 { The rook tidies up, and the open column, the one road into his camp, goes unclaimed. [%cal Gd1d7] }) (15. a4 { Pawn to a4 claims the same square the knight wanted, but a pawn cannot hop into b6 or c5 the way a knight can. } 15... Rb8 16. a5 c4 { Level: the pawn claims the square, and a pawn can never land on d7 the way a rook can. [%cal Gd1d7] }) (15. Nd4 { Knight to d4 jumps in, but the pawn on c5 simply takes it. } 15... cxd4 { A whole knight lost for nothing. [%csl Rd4] }) 15... Bb7 16. Kf2 Rac8 17. Rd7 { The rook stands on the seventh row, his bishop is still biting on the pawn wall, and every piece he owns is tied to defence. [%csl Gd7] } 1-0


[Event "Strategy: space, the breakthrough"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Hc5NIk9A/C2dTNu1p"]
[FEN "2kr3r/pp3pp1/2p1pnp1/2n5/2P3P1/P2PP3/1BK1BP1P/R6R b - - 1 16"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/2kr3r/pp3pp1/2p1pnp1/2n5/2P3P1/P2PP3/1BK1BP1P/R6R_b_-_-_1_16"]

{ The space plan already has his king side pawns stuck, and your bishop on b2 wants the long road thrown open. Which pawn breaks through? [%cal Gb2h8] }
16... g5 { He locks the king side shut with g5. The h column is his unless you break it open by force. [%csl Rg5] } 17. h4 { Pawn to h4! He is forced to take, and pushing g5 next shuts his knight out for good. The bishop on b2 then owns the whole long diagonal. [%cal Gh4g5,Gb2h8] } (17. h3 { Pawn to h3 only goes half the distance, and nothing about the position actually changes. } 17... Rh7 18. Rag1 Rdh8 { Half a step, and he doubles rooks on the h column to meet the break when it finally comes. [%cal Gh2h4] }) (17. Bd4 { Bishop to d4 hits the knight on c5, and he simply steps it aside. } 17... Ncd7 18. h4 Rxh4 { A tempo won, and once he steps aside the break costs a pawn to force through. [%cal Gh2h4] }) (17. Be5 { Bishop to e5 jumps into the centre, but it gives him time to swing his rook to h3. } 17... Rh3 18. Bg3 Re8 { The bishop centralises well, and his rook reaches h3 and starts making noise of its own. [%cal Gh2h4] }) (17. Rab1 { Rook to b1 doubles on a column that has nothing on it to attack. } 17... Rh3 18. Be5 Nfd7 { Equal: the rook heads down an empty column, and his own rook claims h3 completely for free. [%csl Rb1] }) (17. Bxf6 { Trading on f6 gives up your best bishop, the very one the break was meant to unleash. } 17... gxf6 18. d4 Nd7 { He is the one pressing now: his king side is safe, and the bishop that wanted that road is gone. [%csl Rb2] }) 17... gxh4 { He is forced to take on h4. Now shut the door on his knight. } 18. g5 { The pawn still breaks through a move later, and he spends the free move doubling his own rooks. [%cal Gg4g5] } (18. Rag1 { Rook to g1 also works, doubling first before the pawn push. } 18... Rdg8 19. g5 Nfd7 { Fine, just slower: the bishop chases the knight first and the break lands a move behind schedule. [%cal Gg4g5] }) (18. Bd4 { Bishop to d4 chases the knight back first, before playing the pawn break. } 18... Ncd7 19. g5 Ne8 { A natural pawn move, and he doubles rooks on the g column while you play it. [%cal Gg4g5] }) (18. f4 { Pawn to f4 is a useful central move, but it is not the urgent one here. } 18... Rdg8 19. Rh2 Nfd7 { The rook lines up first, and he matches you column for column before the pawn ever moves. [%cal Gg4g5] }) (18. Rh2 { Rook to h2 lines up on the h column first, before playing the pawn break. } 18... Rh7 19. Rah1 Rdh8 { Only a little better for you: the rook lines up first, but he matches you column for column before the pawn ever moves. }) 1-0


[Event "Strategy: space, invade on the open column"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/Hc5NIk9A/uDudRkx6"]
[FEN "1n1q1rk1/Nb2ppbp/pp4p1/3p4/3Pn3/BP1BPN2/P3QPPP/2R3K1 w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/1n1q1rk1/Nb2ppbp/pp4p1/3p4/3Pn3/BP1BPN2/P3QPPP/2R3K1_w_-_-_0_1"]

{ The space plan has paid off: the c column is open and yours, but a rook alone is not enough to break through. Bring the queen up behind it, and invade. }
1. Qc2 { Queen to c2 completes the battery down the c column, with the square c7 as the target. [%cal Gc2c7][%csl Gc7] } (1. h4 { Pawn to h4 first also works: the queen still reaches c2, one move slower. } 1... b5 2. Qc2 Qb6 { Winning still: the queen lands on c2 anyway, a move later than going straight there. [%cal Gd1c2] }) (1. h3 { Pawn to h3 is a useful little move, and it does nothing to get your queen behind the rook. } 1... b5 2. Qc2 Qb6 { Winning still: she reaches c2 all the same, one tempo behind schedule. [%cal Gd1c2] }) (1. Qd1 { Queen to d1 heads the wrong way entirely, away from the column she is wanted on. } 1... b5 2. Qc2 Qb6 { Clearly better still: she comes to c2 in the end, a move later than she needed to. [%cal Gd1c2] }) (1. Bb4 { Bishop to b4 develops, and he simply kicks it away with f6. } 1... f6 2. Qc2 a5 { Clearly better, and slower: the bishop trip costs a tempo the direct plan never needs. [%cal Gd1c2] }) (1. Bxe7 { Taking the pawn on e7 looks free at first glance. } 1... Qxe7 { It is not: his queen simply recaptures, and you have given up a whole bishop for a single pawn. [%csl Re7] }) 1... a5 { He pushes his pawn to a5. Now jump the knight into the centre. } 2. Ne5 { Knight to e5 blocks his bishop's diagonal and attacks the knight on e4 at the same time. [%csl Ge5] } (2. Qc7 { Queen to c7 also works: straight into the invasion, right now. } 2... Qxc7 3. Rxc7 Ba6 { Winning: your rook lands on the seventh row and the invasion is already complete. }) (2. h3 { Pawn to h3 is slow: it does not challenge the knight on e4, and it does not block his bishop either. } 2... Bf6 3. Qc7 Ba6 { Winning still: c7 falls to the invasion anyway, without first dealing with his pieces in the centre. [%cal Gf3e5] }) (2. h4 { Pawn to h4 is slow: a king-side pawn move that leaves the centre exactly as it was. } 2... Bf6 3. h5 Na6 { Winning still: the pawn storm rolls on, and his pieces get a free move to reorganise. [%cal Gf3e5] }) (2. g3 { Pawn to g3 shores the king up a little, and does nothing about the knight on e4. } 2... Bf6 3. Qc7 Ba6 { Winning still: c7 still falls, one move later than it needed to. [%cal Gf3e5] }) 2... Bxe5 { He trades on e5. Recapture with the pawn. } 3. dxe5 { The pawn recaptures, and it shuts his own knight in behind it. } (3. Qc7 { Queen to c7 jumps in a move too soon, and his bishop simply eats it. } 3... Bxc7 { A whole queen lost for nothing. [%csl Rc7] }) (3. Bxe4 { Taking the knight on e4 wins a piece, but his bishop escapes through the trades that follow. } 3... Bd6 4. Bxd6 Qxd6 { Only a little better: everything trades off and the invasion loses most of its bite. [%cal Gc2c7] }) 3... Ng5 { The knight retreats to g5. Now finish the invasion. } 4. Qc7 { Queen to c7! He is forced to trade, and your rook lands on the seventh row behind it. [%cal Gc7d8,Gc1c7] } (4. f4 { Pushing f4 kicks the knight first. Also good, just slower. } 4... Ne6 5. f5 Nc5 { Winning still: the knight gets kicked back the same way, and the attack rolls on a move slower. [%cal Gc2c7] }) (4. Bb5 { Bishop to b5 develops, but it hits nothing useful from there. } 4... Ne6 5. f4 Kh8 { Winning still, just slower — the bishop trip adds nothing at all to the attack. [%cal Gc2c7] }) (4. Be2 { Bishop to e2 is purely defensive, and it does nothing to kick the knight off g5. } 4... Ne6 5. Bb5 d4 { Clearly better still: the bishop reaches b5 in the end, a tempo later than going there directly. [%cal Gc2c7] }) (4. h4 { Pawn to h4 also kicks the knight away. } 4... Ne6 5. Bb5 d4 { Clearly better still: the knight gets kicked, and the bishop and the break both arrive a move late. [%cal Gc2c7] }) 1-0
